%I A366998 #37 Oct 31 2026 12:29:45
%S A366998 0,1,4,12,28,8,124,128,263,9,1303,519707,380,3435
%N A366998 a(n) is the numerator of the maximum expected number of steps until a random walker falls into the sea when walking on the cells of an n-celled polyomino island.
%C A366998 The random walker is not aware of where the safe cells (cells of the island) are; it just moves randomly to one of the four possible neighbors.
%C A366998 For all n <= 13, except for n = 3, the optimal island (with a designated starting cell) is unique up to translations, rotations, and reflections. See linked illustration.
%C A366998 From _Pontus von Brömssen_, Oct 30 2026: (Start)
%C A366998 The following simulated annealing search method seems to produce good candidates for optimal islands. Fix the starting cell (0,0) and pick an arbitrary initial n-celled island around it. To get a new candidate from the current island, start a random walk at (0,0) and run it until it leaves the island. Add the cell that would have saved the walker at the last step to the new island, and delete a cell randomly chosen from those not used by the walker (or from all cells except the starting cell if all cells were used). (The new island is allowed to be disconnected.) Keep the new island if the exact expected length of the walk is longer than for the current island; if the expected length is shorter, the new island is kept with a probability that depends on the difference in expected lengths and the current temperature. This method, with a suitable cooling schedule, quickly finds the optimal solutions for n <= 13 (even without cooling) and suggests the following values of a(n)/A366999(n) for n = 14..30 (the values are definitely lower bounds):
%C A366998    n | lower bound for a(n)/A366999(n)
