%C A366998   14 |            500/83
%C A366998   15 |          12720/1987
%C A366998   16 |            236/35
%C A366998   17 |      911479056/127613381
%C A366998   18 |        1433772/189707
%C A366998   19 |         370383/46679
%C A366998   20 |        9130983/1086383
%C A366998   21 |              9
%C A366998   22 |        2116017/226825
%C A366998   23 |         306855/31727
%C A366998   24 |         311310/31067
%C A366998   25 |            135/13
%C A366998   26 |       25900965/2443831
%C A366998   27 |  1814945284519/166152427907
%C A366998   28 |     1292684865/114332371
%C A366998   29 | 72919987162377/6315639892733
%C A366998   30 |        1989503/167091
%C A366998 (End)
%H A366998 Pontus von Brömssen, <a href="/A366998/a366998.svg">Illustration of the optimal islands and starting cells (black dots) for n = 1..13</a>.
%H A366998 Pontus von Brömssen, <a href="https://oeis.org/plot2a?name1=A366998&amp;name2=A366999&amp;tform1=untransformed&amp;tform2=untransformed&amp;shift=0&amp;radiop1=ratio&amp;drawpoints=true">Plot of a(n)/A366999(n) vs n</a>, using Plot2.
%H A366998 <a href="/index/Pol#polyominoes">Index entries for sequences related to polyominoes</a>.
%e A366998 For n = 0, the random walker falls into the sea immediately, so a(0) = 0.
%e A366998 For n = 1, the starting cell is the only cell of the island, so the random walker falls into the sea after the first step and a(1) = 1.
%e A366998 For n = 2, the island consists of the starting cell and one adjacent cell, so the probability that the random walker stays on the island is 1/4 at each step. This implies that the expected number of steps is 4/3, so a(2) = 4. (The number of steps follows a geometric distribution.)
%Y A366998 Cf. A000105, A365964, A366999 (denominators), A369368 (polyhexes), A369370 (polyiamonds), A397795 (polyknights).
